===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|seysen|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|seisin|lang=fro}}, from the verb {{term|seisir|lang=fro}}, from {{etyl|VL.|en}} {{term/t|la|*saciō}}, from the same Proto-Indo-European root as {{etyl|got|-}} {{term|𐍃𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽|tr=satjan|lang=got}} and Old English {{term|settan|lang=ang}}. More at {{l|en|seize}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|legal|common law|historical|lang=en}} A [[feudal]] term for an [[entitlement]] to a [[freehold]] estate with a right to immediate possession; still used in technical discussions of real property law today.
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} The act of taking possession.
# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} The thing possessed; [[property]].
#: {{rfquotek|Sir M. Hale}}

